# Helsinki UDBMS Person: document and graph, which is the split the publisher ships.
#
# There is no relational side. Patent has one and Person does not, and manufacturing it by moving
# the page collection into PostgreSQL would be exactly the design decision these instances exist to
# avoid: what makes them evidence is that nobody here chose which model holds what.
#
# The correspondence this instance carries is a *natural identifier* rather than a registry key.
# A page title in the document side names the same entity as a subject in the graph, spelled
# differently -- "Henry Standing Bear" against "Henry_Standing_Bear" -- so it holds at 0.0089 under
# equality and 0.999967 once spaces and underscores are treated alike. Patent offers no such case;
# there, equality suffices. Both spellings happen to be present on the document side, so the same
# fact is reachable under equality through `normalized_from` and only under a comparison relation
# through `title`, which is what makes it a controlled comparison rather than an anecdote.

mongo        mongo  hperson  pages

neo4j-label  graph  neo4j    HPerson

# The triple store's predicates, exported as one relationship scope. Objects that are RDF literals
# were set aside at build time: a literal is a value, not an entity, and 608,081 of this file's
# triples have one, so admitting them would fill the graph with values masquerading as nodes.
neo4j-rels   graph  neo4j    MENTIONS
